---
title: Different Scales, Shared Adaptability
authors: [Lewis Lan]
broadcast: 5
order: 3
---

The ten organizations analyzed are independent nonprofit arts and media institutions operating across New York City. Although they share similar cultural ambitions—producing exhibitions, performances, broadcasts, publications, and public programs—their financial structures differ dramatically. In 2024, annual revenue ranged from just $64,620 at Cornell Media Guild and $157,000 at Carriage Trade to $43.3 million at the Museum of Chinese in the Americas (MOCA). Total assets show an equally wide range, from only $24,300 at Carriage Trade to $57.4 million at the Museum of Chinese in the Americas. What becomes especially clear is that institutional activity does not correspond neatly to financial scale or annual surplus. BRIC generated about $13.3 million in revenue while spending $18 million, a difference of roughly $4.7 million. Swiss Institute similarly reported $4.9 million in revenue against $7.5 million in expenses. Yet both retain substantial asset bases of approximately $15.9 million for BRIC and $4.9 million for Swiss Institute, granting annual programming to operate within a much larger financial structure. Other organizations function very differently: Carriage Trade spent roughly $153,000 against $157,000 in revenue, while Montez Press Arts brought in $480,000 and spent $389,000, demonstrating much leaner operating models.

{% galeria %}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/lewis-09.png" alt="Bar chart of 2024 revenue for ten New York arts and media nonprofits, from under $100,000 at Cornell Media Guild to over $40 million at the Museum of Chinese in the Americas" pie="1.1. Revenue 2024" /%}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/lewis-10.png" alt="Bar chart of 2024 expenses for the same ten organizations, with BRIC the largest at about $18 million" pie="1.2. Expenses 2024" /%}
{% /galeria %}

{% galeria %}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/lewis-11.png" alt="Bar chart of 2024 total assets for the ten organizations, with the Museum of Chinese in the Americas far ahead at over $55 million" pie="1.3. Assets 2024" /%}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/lewis-12.png" alt="Bar chart of 2024 total liabilities for the ten organizations, with BRIC, Swiss Institute, WFMU and MOCA each above $3 million" pie="1.4. Liabilities 2024" /%}
{% /galeria %}

The comparison therefore reveals not one model for a successful cultural institution, but many. Some sustain programming through significant assets and liabilities, while others operate with remarkably little permanent financial infrastructure. For smaller institutions especially, this raises an architectural question of how much permanent space and overhead must an organization maintain to produce culture? An elastic model of keeping a small permanent core while expanding through partnerships, shared facilities, and temporary occupation could allow programming capacity to grow without requiring the institution itself to expand permanently.

